WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.493
Flip the derivative

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.519
shrink the math

00:00:03.054 --> 00:00:04.103
Dan Piponi

00:00:04.139 --> 00:00:05.094
the blogger known

00:00:04.905 --> 00:00:05.660
as sigfpe

00:00:05.837 --> 00:00:06.686
offers a new

00:00:06.497 --> 00:00:07.228
way to think

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.962
about automatic differentiation

00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:10.730
inspired by Brzozowski

00:00:10.542 --> 00:00:11.957
derivatives for regular

00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:13.018
expression matching

00:00:13.558 --> 00:00:14.489
He pairs two

00:00:14.301 --> 00:00:15.315
polynomials

00:00:15.409 --> 00:00:16.164
using one as

00:00:15.975 --> 00:00:17.366
a differential operator

00:00:17.178 --> 00:00:17.909
on the other

00:00:18.048 --> 00:00:18.885
The pairing is

00:00:18.697 --> 00:00:19.746
symmetric so a

00:00:19.558 --> 00:00:20.984
complicated operator

00:00:20.796 --> 00:00:21.550
can act on

00:00:21.362 --> 00:00:22.552
a simple expression

00:00:22.364 --> 00:00:23.118
instead

00:00:23.654 --> 00:00:24.880
Intermediate results

00:00:24.692 --> 00:00:25.623
stay small

00:00:25.753 --> 00:00:27.026
computing a 2D

00:00:26.838 --> 00:00:28.453
Laplacian needs only

00:00:28.265 --> 00:00:29.797
4 tracked coefficients

00:00:29.928 --> 00:00:30.623
not a full

00:00:30.435 --> 00:00:31.413
Hessian and the

00:00:31.225 --> 00:00:32.404
idea extends to

00:00:32.215 --> 00:00:33.642
other constant differential

00:00:33.454 --> 00:00:34.326
operators

00:00:34.854 --> 00:00:35.868
Full story on

00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:36.575
the dashboard
